			}
			h2 {
				font-family: 'Libre Baskerville', serif;
				font-size: 22px;
				font-weight: 700;
				margin-top: 2.5rem;
				margin-bottom: 1rem;
			}
			p {
				margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
			}
			code {
				font-family: monospace;
				background: #f5f5f5;
				padding: 2px 5px;
			}
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<h1>Stop Skidding</h1>
		
		<p>
			You're here because you copy-paste code you don't understand. You download "hacking tools" thinking it makes you dangerous. You're not. You're a script kiddie and everyone can tell.
		</p>

		<p>
			Running someone else's exploit doesn't make you a hacker. You can't write a function. You can't debug an error. You don't know what a compiler does. You just run <code>python exploit.py</code> and hope.
		</p>

		<p>
			Real developers spot you immediately. You ask questions Google answered years ago. You never read documentation. You don't understand the code you're running. You want results without work.
		</p>

		<p>
			Nobody respects you. Not developers. Not security researchers. Not other script kiddies. You're a joke in every Discord, every forum, every IRC channel. People mock you when you're not looking.
		</p>

		<h2>What You Think vs Reality</h2>

		<p>
			You think you're accessing mainframes and bypassing firewalls. In reality you're stuck on "module not found" errors and permission denied messages. You're Googling why code from 2015 doesn't work. You're using deprecated Python versions because you don't understand dependencies.
		</p>

		<h2>Why This Fails</h2>

		<p>
			Tools aren't knowledge. When your exploit breaks—and it will break—you're helpless. You can't fix it. You can't modify it. You don't know why it failed. So you download another tool and repeat the cycle.
		</p>

		<p>
			Every question you ask reveals your incompetence. Every forum post broadcasts that you don't belong. You've spent months or years on this and still can't code a basic script from scratch.
		</p>

		<h2>The Fix</h2>

		<p>
			Stop pretending. Learn to code. Pick a language—Python, Java, C, doesn't matter. Work through a real curriculum. Learn variables, loops, functions, data structures. Write code from scratch. Build things. Debug your own errors.
		</p>

		<p>
			If security interests you, learn how systems work. Study networking, operating systems, databases. You can't exploit what you don't understand. This takes years, not weekends.
		</p>

		<p>
			You can keep downloading tools and learning nothing, or you can develop actual skills people respect. Your choice.
		</p>

		<p>
			Now close this tab and read some documentation.
		</p>
	</body>
</html>
